#N canvas 20 20 7000 19280 12;
#X text 20 20 launchpad-bench -- the Launchpad acceptance run: the grid \, the grid arbiter \, the mode bus and the first c_clock instance. STEPPED BY HAND: press GO to run the step that has just been described \, press GO again to describe the next one. Nothing moves until you ask it to \, so the PASS IF is on screen and STILL while you read it. The prompt line always says what the next press will do., f 120;
#X text 20 150 GO ON THE MAC: the bng below \, or the dev panel's ENCODER CLICK -- u_mother-stub sends encbut unconditionally and nothing in Cut It consumes it. TURNING the encoder repeats the current step without advancing \, which is what to do when you looked away., f 120;
#X text 2400 200 ⚠️ GO ON THE DEVICE IS NOT THE ENCODER -- IT IS netcat \, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT OPTIONAL. mother only forwards encbut once a patch has asked with /enableEncoder \, and NOTHING IN Cut It EVER ASKS -- m_organelle leaves the encoder out on purpose. So the click that drives this bench on the Mac is silently dead on the Organelle. Send instead with ./test/run.sh --bench <name> from the Mac. ⚠️ NOT netcat: the line this file used to print does nothing on macOS -- BSD nc exits before the datagram is flushed at -w0 \, and -w1 was measured to fail too \, while the port IS bound and the bench is fine. It looks exactly like a dead bench. The device cannot send to itself either: busybox here has no nc at all. Asking mother to enable the encoder is NOT the fix either: that means writing to oscOut \, and g_oled is its sole owner., f 120;
#X text 20 280 Load it as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It touches nothing in the deployed patch -- it only pushes onto the same buses a controller would. A PASS IF is printed for EVERY step including the ones whose correct result is that nothing happens \, which are otherwise impossible to mark off., f 120;
#X text 2400 20 ON THE MAC EVERY ERROR STEP ALSO PRINTS /sdcard/cut-it-err.cur: write failed \, AND THAT IS NOT A FAILED STEP. u_err keeps its durable log on the Organelle's SD card \, which no Mac has -- so the write is attempted and refused every time. It is in fact the only proof off-device that the logging path ran at all. On the device that line must NOT appear \, and if it does the card is unwritable and the log is being lost silently., f 120;
#X obj 6000 40 r \$0-say;
#X obj 6000 100 print;
#X text 20 360 GO -- click here \, or the dev panel's encoder button. MAC ONLY -- on the device use netcat \, top right., f 60;
#X obj 20 420 bng 40 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 20 500 s \$0-go;
#X obj 300 420 r encbut;
#X obj 300 480 select 1;
#X obj 300 540 t b;
#X obj 300 600 s \$0-go;
#X text 560 480 encbut sends 1 THEN 0 \, so select 1 is what stops one press counting twice., f 46;
#X obj 300 680 netreceive 9998 1;
#X obj 300 740 route go rerun;
#X obj 300 800 t b;
#X obj 300 860 s \$0-go;
#X obj 620 800 t b;
#X obj 620 860 s \$0-rerun;
#X text 860 800 rerun fires the CURRENT step again without advancing \, so a visual step can be looked at more than once. [r]epeat in test/run.sh sends it., f 46;
#X obj 1400 420 r enc;
#X obj 1400 480 t b;
#X obj 1400 540 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 1660 480 enc is 1 for up and 0 for down -- not plus or minus one -- so either direction repeats., f 46;
#X text 20 920 THE STATE MACHINE. phase 0 means the step has been described and not yet run \; phase 1 means it has run. SETTING THE STEP NUMBER IS WHAT DESCRIBES IT \, so advancing is just an increment -- there is no second path to keep in step., f 120;
#X obj 20 980 r \$0-go;
#X obj 20 1040 f;
#X obj 20 1100 select 0 1;
#X obj 300 1040 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1170 t b b;
#X msg 300 1230 1;
#X obj 300 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1290 f;
#X obj 20 1350 s \$0-do-run;
#X obj 20 1230 r \$0-rerun;
#X obj 900 1170 t b b;
#X msg 1180 1230 0;
#X obj 1180 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 900 1350 f;
#X obj 900 1410 + 1;
#X obj 900 1470 s \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 980 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 1040 t f f f;
#X obj 1700 1110 s \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 1980 1110 s \$0-set-run;
#X obj 1180 1350 r \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 300 1410 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2260 1110 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2100 980 r \$0-repeat;
#X obj 2100 1040 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2500 980 loadbang;
#X obj 2500 1040 del 500;
#X msg 2500 1100 1;
#X obj 2500 1160 s \$0-set-step;
#X text 20 1660 BEAT COUNTERS. \$0-zero resets one and starts a 10 SECOND window \; the count is latched and printed when that window closes \, and \$0-read reprints the latched value. THE WINDOW IS MACHINE-TIMED ON PURPOSE -- under manual stepping the gap between two steps is however long you take to judge one \, so a count taken between steps would mean nothing. On the Mac with DSP OFF every count reads 0 \, which looks exactly like a dead clock rather than a setting., f 120;
#X obj 20 1860 r clock;
#X obj 20 1930 t b;
#X obj 20 2000 f;
#X obj 180 2060 + 1;
#X obj 180 2120 t f f;
#X obj 420 2180 f;
#X obj 720 1860 r \$0-zero;
#X obj 720 1930 t b b;
#X msg 900 2000 0;
#X obj 720 2060 del 10000;
#X obj 420 2240 t f f;
#X obj 720 2300 f -1;
#X obj 20 2380 print BEATS;
#X obj 970 2240 r \$0-read;
#X text 20 2440 latch starts at -1 \, so a count read before its 10 seconds are up says so rather than lying., f 46;
#X text 20 2580 DESCRIBING A STEP. One select per step rather than one wide fan \, so every cord stays short and local -- the same reason u_map chains its routes., f 120;
#X obj 20 2680 r \$0-do-show;
#X obj 20 2740 select 1;
#X obj 240 2740 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2740 === STEP-01-of-25 === Baseline -- 120 BPM -- stopped -- compose mode-1;
#X msg 700 2790 PASS IF: The top row shows one bright green lamp at the far left and five dim ones beside it. The bottom row of pads has a single white pad. Dim means faint and not off. The white pad is already walking and that is correct -- a frozen pad is the failure. Everything else on the surface is dark.;
#X msg 700 2840 >>> press GO to run step 1 of 25;
#X obj 240 2910 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3000 select 2;
#X obj 240 3000 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3000 === STEP-02-of-25 === Mode -- selecting the fourth mode;
#X msg 700 3050 PASS IF: The bright lamp moves to the fourth position and the other five go dim. Nothing else on the grid changes.;
#X msg 700 3100 >>> press GO to run step 2 of 25;
#X obj 240 3170 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3260 select 3;
#X obj 240 3260 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3260 === STEP-03-of-25 === Mode -- the second lamp;
#X msg 700 3310 PASS IF: The bright lamp lands on the second position.;
#X msg 700 3360 >>> press GO to run step 3 of 25;
#X obj 240 3430 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3520 select 4;
#X obj 240 3520 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3520 === STEP-04-of-25 === Mode -- the fifth lamp;
#X msg 700 3570 PASS IF: The bright lamp lands on the fifth position and the other five go dim.;
#X msg 700 3620 >>> press GO to run step 4 of 25;
#X obj 240 3690 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3780 select 5;
#X obj 240 3780 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3780 === STEP-05-of-25 === Mode -- the sixth and last lamp;
#X msg 700 3830 PASS IF: The bright lamp lands on the sixth and last position.;
#X msg 700 3880 >>> press GO to run step 5 of 25;
#X obj 240 3950 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4040 select 6;
#X obj 240 4040 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4040 === STEP-06-of-25 === The grid selector must not reach the OLED -- sending grid vocabulary g_grid ignores;
#X msg 700 4090 PASS IF: Nothing happens on either surface. In particular no parameter row called grid appears on the OLED.;
#X msg 700 4140 >>> press GO to run step 6 of 25;
#X obj 240 4210 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4300 select 7;
#X obj 240 4300 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4300 === STEP-07-of-25 === A modal -- the whole surface claimed;
#X msg 700 4350 PASS IF: Every pad and every lamp on the top row turns blue. The mode lamps are covered too. The OLED must not change.;
#X msg 700 4400 >>> press GO to run step 7 of 25;
#X obj 240 4470 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4560 select 8;
#X obj 240 4560 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4560 === STEP-08-of-25 === A mode change underneath a modal -- nothing should be visible;
#X msg 700 4610 PASS IF: Nothing happens. The surface stays blue. The mode really does change underneath and you will see it two steps from now.;
#X msg 700 4660 >>> press GO to run step 8 of 25;
#X obj 240 4730 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4820 select 9;
#X obj 240 4820 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4820 === STEP-09-of-25 === An alert on top of a modal -- two layers deep;
#X msg 700 4870 PASS IF: The surface turns red for about two seconds and then goes back to blue -- not to the mode lamps.;
#X msg 700 4920 >>> press GO to run step 9 of 25;
#X obj 240 4990 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5080 select 10;
#X obj 240 5080 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5080 === STEP-10-of-25 === Modal off;
#X msg 700 5130 PASS IF: The grid returns to mode lamps and the beat row. The bright lamp is now the third one -- the change made while the modal covered it.;
#X msg 700 5180 >>> press GO to run step 10 of 25;
#X obj 240 5250 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5340 select 11;
#X obj 240 5340 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5340 === STEP-11-of-25 === An alert -- a fail -- which outranks everything;
#X msg 700 5390 PASS IF: The whole surface turns red and then goes back to the mode lamps by itself after about two seconds. A grid that stays red is the failure.;
#X msg 700 5440 >>> press GO to run step 11 of 25;
#X obj 240 5510 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5600 select 12;
#X obj 240 5600 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5600 === STEP-12-of-25 === An alert -- a warn -- which the grid must ignore but the OLED must show;
#X msg 700 5650 PASS IF: Nothing happens on the grid and the OLED does show the warning.;
#X msg 700 5700 >>> press GO to run step 12 of 25;
#X obj 240 5770 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 5860 select 13;
#X obj 240 5860 t b b b;
#X msg 700 5860 === STEP-13-of-25 === The 30 second modal safety TTL -- this step needs you to wait;
#X msg 700 5910 PASS IF: The surface turns green and then clears itself about thirty seconds later with nothing sent to it. Do not press GO -- sit and watch it.;
#X msg 700 5960 >>> press GO to run step 13 of 25;
#X obj 240 6030 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6120 select 14;
#X obj 240 6120 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6120 === STEP-14-of-25 === Transport -- start -- and the beat counter starts with it;
#X msg 700 6170 PASS IF: The white pad walks along the bottom row twice a second and the aux LED goes green. On the Mac the aux LED is not A button -- it is the numeric readout labelled aux-LED on the bottom row of the dev panel -- with a symbol box beside it spelling the colour. On the Mac tick enable-DSP first or the pad will not move. A BEATS line prints about ten seconds from now.;
#X msg 700 6220 >>> press GO to run step 14 of 25 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 6290 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6380 select 15;
#X obj 240 6380 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6380 === STEP-15-of-25 === The beat row wrapping -- watch only -- this step sends nothing on purpose;
#X msg 700 6430 PASS IF: The white pad reaches the eighth pad and the next step is back to the first -- with no gap and no stray light anywhere else. Nothing is sent for this step. Just watch one wrap go by.;
#X msg 700 6480 >>> press GO to run step 15 of 25;
#X obj 240 6550 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6640 select 16;
#X obj 240 6640 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6640 === STEP-16-of-25 === Tempo -- 240 BPM -- so the beat row should double;
#X msg 700 6690 PASS IF: The white pad moves twice as fast. The BEATS line just printed should read about 20 beats and the next one about 40 beats. Expect A visible swing here and do not fail the step for it -- the row can only move on a 100 ms boundary so at 240 BPM it swings either way by 50 ms.;
#X msg 700 6740 >>> press GO to run step 16 of 25 -- then WAIT for the printed count before pressing GO again;
#X obj 240 6810 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 6900 select 17;
#X obj 240 6900 t b b b;
#X msg 700 6900 === STEP-17-of-25 === Back to 120 and stopped;
#X msg 700 6950 PASS IF: The second BEATS line reads about 40 and then the beat row slows to two a second. The pad must keep walking after the stop.;
#X msg 700 7000 >>> press GO to run step 17 of 25;
#X obj 240 7070 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 7160 select 18;
#X obj 240 7160 t b b b;
#X msg 700 7160 === STEP-18-of-25 === The Launchpad -- press pads and release them;
#X msg 700 7210 PASS IF: Every pad you press reports pad-NN on the OLED with its velocity. Releasing it reports the same name with a velocity of 0 instead. Numbering runs from 11 at the bottom left to 88 at the top right. Pressure on a held pad reports nothing and that is correct.;
#X msg 700 7260 >>> press GO to run step 18 of 25;
#X obj 240 7330 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 7420 select 19;
#X obj 240 7420 t b b b;
#X msg 700 7420 === STEP-19-of-25 === The Launchpad ring -- and the row that must be lit;
#X msg 700 7470 PASS IF: The ring buttons report lp-cc-NN. Check the top left corner reads 90 and the bottom row runs from 101 to 108 in order. CC 1 to 8 must be lit like everything else and must go blue under a modal and red under an alert. One button stays dark -- index 0 -- SETUP. It takes no colour and transmits nothing.;
#X msg 700 7520 >>> press GO to run step 19 of 25;
#X obj 240 7590 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 7680 select 20;
#X obj 240 7680 t b b b;
